gm-method (a 'update sibling SDK / bump a dep' candidate when the consumer postinstall-PATCHES its node_modules copy): a consumer that carries local [marker] patches in node_modules (idempotent postinstall script, anchor-gated) HIDES which fixes are already-present vs upstream-new. To decide a bump safely: (1) diff INSTALLED node_modules/<pkg>/src vs the published-version source IGNORING CRLF (a raw diff flags whole files as changed on line-ending noise alone -- use diff -bw --strip-trailing-cr or grep for the actual semantic markers, else you chase phantom deltas); (2) for each upstream commit in range, classify real-vs-cosmetic by reading the diff, not the changelog (the changelog lists things the consumer ALREADY has as local patches); (3) check the npm REGISTRY latest, not just the sibling checkout -- the published version can be AHEAD of your local source clone, so no publish is needed, just bump+install; (4) confirm the new version is a clean SUPERSET of the local patches (grep the installed-after file for BOTH the perf change AND the correctness invariant the patch protected, e.g. Math.fround parity) so the bump doesn't silently drop a patch while adding the perf win; (5) after install, the obsolete patch-deps bridges warn 'anchor not found' -- that is the SIGNAL the fix upstreamed (the old anchor no longer exists), so REMOVE those dead bridges per the script's own 'remove when upstream ships it' header, leaving a comment witness; (6) witness the perf win bit-identically: run the consumer's own real test suite (placement/collider PARITY tests catch a parity regression) + a node microbench of the hot fn against the new install. The pattern: a 'faster + parity-preserved + patch-now-noop' bump is pure-gain; verify all three before landing, never bump blind.